<Base title="Maps and Hashing · ITC 120" description="Maps and hashing: key-value pairs, put, get, remove, how Java computes a String's hashCode, and how HashMap picks a bucket." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Final › Sets and maps</p>
    <p class="kicker">Sets and maps · Lesson 2 of 2</p>
    <h1>Maps and Hashing in Java</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Store things under a name (a key), and find them again in one jump.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>explain what a map is (<b>key-value pairs</b>, unique keys) and give an everyday example;</li>
        <li>predict what <code>put</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>containsKey</code>, and <code>size</code> return on a Java <code>HashMap</code>, including <code>put</code> on a key that is already there;</li>
        <li>explain how <b>hashing</b> lets <code>get</code> check one bucket instead of every entry;</li>
        <li>compute a short String's <b>hashCode</b> and its <b>bucket index</b> (capacity 16) by hand;</li>
        <li>spot a <b>collision</b> and explain how chaining keeps both keys;</li>
        <li>choose between <code>HashMap</code>, <code>LinkedHashMap</code>, and <code>TreeMap</code> by the order you need.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/sets/'}>Sets lesson</a>, a calculator, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="what">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>What a map is</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of the sari-sari store</p>
        <p>The store owner does not search every shelf for the price of a sachet of coffee. She has a price list: item name (key) → price (value). If the price goes up, she does not add a second line; she <b>crosses out the old price</b> and writes the new one. One item, one price.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={priceList()} /><figcaption>The store's price list works like a map: look up the item (key) to get its price (value).</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Why do we need maps?</h3>
        <p>In <a href={base + '/itc120/arrays/'}>arrays</a> and lists, you find an item by its <b>position</b> (its index), like <code>grades[2]</code>. But in real life you usually know a <b>name or an ID</b>, not a position. To find the grade of student 2025-0101 in a list, you would have to check the items one by one.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={posVsKey()} /><figcaption>A list makes you search by position. A map lets you ask by the key you already know.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>A map fixes this: you store each value under a <b>key</b>, and later you ask for it by that same key.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Map</p>
        <p>A <b>map</b> is an <a href={base + '/itc120/sets/#adt'}>abstract data type</a> (an idea of what you can do, not how it is stored) that stores <b>key-value pairs</b>. Each <b>key</b> is unique and is used to find its <b>value</b>. Maps are also called dictionaries or associative arrays.</p>
        <p>In plain words:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Key</b>: the thing you look things up by (a student ID, an item name). Like a word in a dictionary.</li>
          <li><b>Value</b>: the information stored under that key (a grade, a price). Like the word's meaning.</li>
          <li><b>Key-value pair</b> (also called an <b>entry</b>): one key together with its value, like one line of the price list.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>Maps are everywhere. Here are some examples of keys and the values they lead to:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>→</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>student ID number</td><td>→</td><td>student's name</td></tr>
          <tr><td>product barcode</td><td>→</td><td>price</td></tr>
          <tr><td>contact name</td><td>→</td><td>phone number</td></tr>
          <tr><td>English word</td><td>→</td><td>its meaning</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The two rules</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Keys are unique.</b> Each key appears once. If you store a value under a key that already exists, the old value is <b>replaced</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Values can repeat.</b> Two different students can have the same grade. Two products can have the same price.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p>A <Peek label="set" title="From the Sets lesson" html={setPeek} /> from the last lesson is like a map with only keys: it answers "is it there?", while a map answers "is it there, and what is its value?"</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · In code</p>
      <h2>Maps in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Java's <code>Map</code> interface (the list of methods every map class must have) has these main operations:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>put(key, value)</code></td><td><b>insert</b> a pair, or <b>replace</b> the value if the key exists; returns the old value (<code>null</code> if the key was new)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>get(key)</code></td><td><b>look up</b> the value; returns <code>null</code> (Java's word for "nothing") if the key is missing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>remove(key)</code></td><td><b>delete</b> the pair with that key; returns its value (<code>null</code> if the key was missing)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>containsKey(key)</code></td><td><code>true</code> if the key is in the map</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>size()</code></td><td>number of pairs</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Like sets, Java has three main map types: <code>HashMap</code> (no guaranteed order, the usual choice; Part 3 explains the "hash"), <code>LinkedHashMap</code> (keeps the order keys were first added), and <code>TreeMap</code> (sorted by key, built on a <a href={base + '/itc120/red-black-trees/'}>red-black tree</a>). The end of this part compares them.</p>
      <p>The picture below shows the most important rule of <code>put</code>: when the key is already there, the value is replaced and the map does not grow.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={putReplace()} /><figcaption><code>put</code> on an existing key replaces the value and returns the old one.</figcaption></figure>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(basics)} /></pre>
      <TryIt cls="Grades" />
      <p class="small"><b>Output</b> (the last two lines can come out in another order; <a href="#no-order">Part 6</a> explains why):</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{gradesOut}</code></pre>
      <p>Here is how to read the new lines aloud:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl ra">
        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; grades</code></td><td>"grades is a map from String keys to Integer values"</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>= new HashMap&lt;&gt;()</code></td><td>"make a new, empty HashMap" (the empty <code>&lt;&gt;</code> copies the types from the left side)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>grades.put("2025-0012", 89)</code></td><td>"store 89 under the key 2025-0012"</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>grades.get("2025-0012")</code></td><td>"give me the value stored under 2025-0012"</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>for (String id : grades.keySet())</code></td><td>"for each key id in the map's keys"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="small muted">Why <code>Integer</code> and not <code>int</code>? The types inside <code>&lt; &gt;</code> must be classes. <code>Integer</code> is the class version of <code>int</code>, and Java converts between the two for you, so <code>put("2025-0012", 89)</code> just works. (You first met this in the <a href={base + '/itc120/stacks/#why-integer'}>Stacks</a> lesson.)</p>
      <div class="sub" id="map-kinds">
        <h3>HashMap, LinkedHashMap, or TreeMap?</h3>
        <p>All three classes have the same <code>Map</code> methods, so <code>put</code>, <code>get</code>, and <code>remove</code> work the same way in each. They differ in one thing you can see: the <b>order</b> of the keys when you loop over the map or print it. This program puts the same 4 pairs, in the same order, into one map of each kind:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(threeMaps)} /></pre>
        <TryIt cls="ThreeMaps" />
        <p class="small"><b>Output</b> (checked in Java). Java prints a map as <code>key=value</code> pairs inside curly braces:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{threeOut}</code></pre>
        <p>Read the three lines one at a time:</p>
        <ul class="flow">
          <li><b>HashMap</b> printed Liza, Dan, Rico, Bea: neither the order added nor A to Z. It is <b>bucket order</b>, which <a href="#no-order">Part 6</a> explains.</li>
          <li><b>LinkedHashMap</b> printed Rico, Bea, Liza, Dan: the order the keys were <b>first added</b>. The second <code>put("Rico", 91)</code> changed Rico's value but not his place.</li>
          <li><b>TreeMap</b> printed Bea, Dan, Liza, Rico: <b>sorted by key</b>, A to Z.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Here is when to use each one:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x">
          <table class="tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Order of keys</th><th>Built with</th><th>Speed of get and put</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td>no guaranteed order</td><td>a hash table (Parts 3 to 6)</td><td>fastest: about the same few steps, however big the map</td><td>you only look things up by key, like student ID → grade. The usual choice.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td>the order keys were first added</td><td>a hash table + a linked list through the entries</td><td>almost as fast as <code>HashMap</code></td><td>the order of arrival matters, like a sign-up sheet: name → time signed up</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>TreeMap</code></td><td>sorted by key</td><td>a <a href={base + '/itc120/red-black-trees/'}>red-black tree</a></td><td>a bit slower: grows with the tree's height (about log n)</td><td>you need the keys sorted, like an alphabetical class list: name → grade</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted">Good to know: a <code>TreeMap</code> sorts String keys by their character codes, so every capital letter comes before every small letter ("Zed" comes before "ana"). Write names the same way, like "Ana" and "Zed", to get A to Z.</p>
        <p>Which map fits? Three quick situations (for self-study, not for grading):</p>
        <Quiz id="quiz-whichmap" questions={whichMap} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="hashing">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · How it is so fast</p>
      <h2>Hashing</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Imagine a map with 10,000 students stored in one long list. To find one ID, you might have to check all 10,000. A <code>HashMap</code> avoids that with a trick called <b>hashing</b>. Here is the same trick in a place you know.</p>
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of the faculty pigeonholes</p>
        <p>The faculty office has 16 pigeonholes for mail, numbered 0 to 15. Instead of one big pile, the office uses a rule that turns each name into a pigeonhole number: add up the letters' places in the alphabet (A = 1, B = 2, …, Z = 26), divide by 16, and keep the remainder. A letter for Tan goes into slot 3. To find Tan's mail later, use the same rule and check <b>one</b> pigeonhole, not the whole pile.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={pigeonWall()} /><figcaption>A made-up office rule turns the name Tan into slot 3. (Java uses a different rule: Parts 4 and 5 show it.)</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Hashing</p>
        <p><b>Hashing</b> means using a fixed rule to turn a key into a number, and using that number to decide where the key is stored. Because the rule never changes, the <b>same key always gives the same number</b>, so you always know where to look.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Hashing comes with a few new words. The table below matches each one to the pigeonhole picture:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th><th>In the picture</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Hash function</b></td><td>the fixed rule that turns a key into a number. In Java, it is the <code>hashCode()</code> method (Part 4).</td><td>the office's rule, step 1</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Hash code</b></td><td>the number the hash function gives for a key</td><td>35</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Hash table</b></td><td>the row of storage slots inside a <code>HashMap</code> (an array)</td><td>the whole wall</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Bucket</b></td><td>one slot of the hash table. It holds the entries (key-value pairs) that belong there.</td><td>one pigeonhole</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Capacity</b></td><td>how many buckets there are. A new <code>HashMap</code> starts with <b>16</b>; when it holds more than 12 entries (3/4 of 16), it doubles to 32 and moves every key to its new bucket.</td><td>16 pigeonholes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Bucket index</b></td><td>the bucket's number, from 0 to capacity − 1, worked out from the hash code</td><td>slot 3 (step 2)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>So <code>put</code> and <code>get</code> both do the same two steps: <b>(1) compute the key's hash code, (2) turn it into a bucket index</b>. Then they go straight to that bucket.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={hashFlow()} /><figcaption>The two steps for the key "CAT". Parts 4 and 5 show how to do each step by hand.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Why <code>get</code> finds what <code>put</code> stored</p>
        <p>Remember: the same key always gives the same number. When you <code>put("CAT", …)</code>, it goes to bucket 6. When you later call <code>get("CAT")</code>, Java computes bucket 6 again and looks there. It never has to look anywhere else.</p>
        <p>The bucket keeps the <b>whole pair</b> (key and value), not just the value. That way, <code>get</code> can check that the key really is "CAT" before giving back its value.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="hashcode">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Step 1 by hand</p>
      <h2>A String's hashCode</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">hashCode</p>
        <p>In Java, every object has a method called <code>hashCode()</code>. It returns an <code>int</code>: that object's <b>hash code</b>. For a <code>String</code>, Java computes it from the string's characters with a fixed formula, so two equal strings always get the same hash code.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Quick review: characters and strings</h3>
        <p>A <b>character</b> (<code>char</code> in Java) is one letter, digit, symbol, or space, written in single quotes: <code>'D'</code>. A <b>string</b> (<code>String</code>) is a sequence of characters in order, written in double quotes: <code>"DOG"</code>. Each character has a position, its <b>index</b>, starting at 0.</p>
        <p>Inside the computer, every character is stored as a number called its <b>character code</b> (from the ASCII or Unicode table). For example, <code>'D'</code> is 68.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={strChars()} /><figcaption>The string "DOG": three characters in order, each stored as a number.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The hashCode rule</h3>
        <p>The goal is to turn the whole string into <b>one number</b> that depends on every character <b>and on their order</b>. We build that number one character at a time and call it <b>h</b> (short for "hash"). Think of h as a running total: the hash code so far.</p>
        <div class="remember">
          <p class="note-label">The hashCode rule</p>
          <p class="formula">h = 0<br />for each character, left to right: <b>h = 31 × h + code</b></p>
          <p>Read it as: "Start with 0. For each character, multiply what you have so far by 31, then add the character's code." When there are no characters left, h is the hash code.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={hornerPic()} /><figcaption>Each row uses the h from the row above. This is the same "CAT" → 66,486 from the <Peek label="Part 3 picture" title="From Part 3" html={flowPeek} />.</figcaption></figure>
        <p><b>Why multiply by 31?</b> If we only added the codes, "DOG" and "GOD" would get the same number, because they have the same letters. Multiplying by 31 before each add makes each character's <b>position</b> matter: "DOG" gives 67,868, but "GOD" gives 70,748. (Java's designers chose 31 because it is an odd prime that mixes the numbers well, and computers can multiply by it quickly.)</p>
        <p class="small muted">Good to know: Java's documentation writes the same rule as one long formula, s[0]·31<sup>n−1</sup> + s[1]·31<sup>n−2</sup> + … + s[n−1], where s[0] is the code of the first character, s[1] the second, and n is the number of characters. The running total gives the same answer in smaller steps.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Character codes you need</h3>
        <p>Uppercase letters go from A = 65 to Z = 90. Lowercase letters are 32 more: a = 97 to z = 122. The digit 0 is 48, and a space is 32.</p>
        <div class="ascii scroll-x">
          <table class="adj"><tbody>
            <tr>{upper.slice(0, 13).map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr>
            <tr>{upper.slice(0, 13).map((c) => <td>{c.charCodeAt(0)}</td>)}</tr>
            <tr>{upper.slice(13).map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr>
            <tr>{upper.slice(13).map((c) => <td>{c.charCodeAt(0)}</td>)}</tr>
          </tbody></table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Worked example: "DOG"</h3>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Char</th><th>Code</th><th>31 × h + code</th><th>New h</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {worked.steps.map((s) => <tr><td class="mono">{s.ch}</td><td class="mono">{s.code}</td><td class="mono">31 × {n(s.before)} + {s.code}</td><td class="mono">{n(s.after)}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><b>"DOG".hashCode() = {n(worked.hash)}</b></p>
        <p class="small muted">Check with the long formula: 68 × 31² + 79 × 31 + 71 = 68 × 961 + 2,449 + 71 = 65,348 + 2,449 + 71 = {n(worked.hash)}. Same answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ol class="trap-list">
          <li><p><b>Uppercase and lowercase are different characters.</b> <code>'C'</code> is 67 but <code>'c'</code> is 99, so "CAT" and "cat" get different hash codes.</p><Fragment set:html={mapTrapPic(1)} /></li>
          <li><p><b>Start with h = 0.</b> The first step is 31 × 0 + code, which is just the first character's code. Don't multiply the first code by 31.</p><Fragment set:html={mapTrapPic(2)} /></li>
          <li><p><b>Long strings can wrap around.</b> A Java <code>int</code> can only go up to 2,147,483,647. Past that, it <b>wraps around</b> to the most negative <code>int</code> (−2,147,483,648) and keeps counting up from there, like an odometer rolling over from 99999 to 00000. That is how a hash code can become negative. Keys of up to 5 letters never get that big, so short keys are safe. Longer ones can wrap, like "Charlie" in Part 7.</p><Fragment set:html={mapTrapPic(3)} /></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="bucket">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Step 2 by hand</p>
      <h2>From hash code to bucket</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Step 1 gave us a hash code, like {n(worked.hash)} for "DOG". Step 2 turns it into a <b>bucket index</b>. Recall from Part 3: a <b>bucket</b> is one slot of the hash table (one <Peek label="pigeonhole" title="From Part 3" html={pigeonPeek} />), and the <b>capacity</b> is how many buckets there are (16). So the bucket index must be a number from <b>0 to 15</b>, but {n(worked.hash)} is far bigger. In this course, we use the same formula as the Java code you will see in Part 7:</p>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">The bucket rule</p>
        <p class="formula">bucket index = hashCode &amp; (capacity − 1)</p>
        <p>With capacity 16: <code>hashCode &amp; 15</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <p>To read this rule, you need two ideas: binary and the bitwise AND.</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Binary and bits</p>
        <p>Computers store numbers in <b>binary</b>: using only the digits 0 and 1. Each digit is called a <b>bit</b>. From the right, the bits are worth 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, … (each place is worth twice the one before). For example, 13 in binary is <b class="mono">1101</b> = 8 + 4 + 0 + 1.</p>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>How to turn a number into binary</summary><div class="flow">
          <p>Divide by 2 again and again, writing down each remainder, until you reach 0. Then read the remainders from the <b>bottom up</b>. For 13:</p>
          <table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Divide</th><th>Answer</th><th>Remainder</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono">13 ÷ 2</td><td class="mono">6</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">6 ÷ 2</td><td class="mono">3</td><td class="mono">0</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">3 ÷ 2</td><td class="mono">1</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">1 ÷ 2</td><td class="mono">0</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
          </tbody></table>
          <p>Bottom up: <b class="mono">1101</b>. Big numbers take many rows, which is why Way 2 below is faster.</p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Bitwise AND (&amp;)</p>
        <p>The <b>bitwise AND</b>, written <code>&amp;</code>, lines up two numbers in binary and compares them bit by bit. The answer has a 1 only where <b>both</b> numbers have a 1; everywhere else it has a 0.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Since 15 in binary is <b>1111</b>, <code>&amp; 15</code> simply <b>keeps the last 4 bits</b> of the hash code and turns all the others into 0.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={andBits()} /><figcaption>"DOG" has hash code 67,868. Lined up bit by bit with 15, only its last 4 bits survive.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Worked example: "DOG" → bucket {workedIdx}</h3>
        <p>There are two ways to get the answer. Both give the same result for a positive hash code.</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="paper flow">
            <p class="note-label">Way 1: binary</p>
            <p>Convert {n(worked.hash)} to binary (divide by 2 repeatedly and read the remainders from bottom to top):</p>
            <p class="mono">{workedBin}</p>
            <p>Keep the last 4 bits: <b class="mono">{workedBin.slice(-4)}</b> = 8 + 4 = <b>{workedIdx}</b>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="paper flow">
            <p class="note-label">Way 2: remainder shortcut</p>
            <p>The last 4 bits are the remainder when you divide by 16:</p>
            <p class="mono">{n(worked.hash)} ÷ 16 = {Math.floor(worked.hash / 16)} remainder <b>{worked.hash % 16}</b></p>
            <p>So the bucket is <b>{workedIdx}</b>. Much faster with a calculator!</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted">Why does this work? 16 = 2<sup>4</sup>, so dividing by 16 is like chopping off the last 4 bits. The chopped-off bits are the remainder. This only works because the capacity is a power of 2 (16, 32, 64…), which is why HashMap capacities always are.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="factcheck remember">
        <p class="note-label">Good to know: Java's real HashMap</p>
        <p>The formula above is what this course uses, and what the Java program in Part 7 prints. The real <code>HashMap</code> adds one small step first: it mixes the hash code's front bits into its last 4 bits, so keys that differ only in their front bits still spread out over the buckets. Then it does <code>&amp; (capacity − 1)</code>. So the bucket Java <i>actually</i> uses inside can be different: "CAT" gets bucket 6 with our formula but bucket 7 inside Java's HashMap. For exams in this course, use the course formula.</p>
        <p class="small muted">For the curious: the extra step is <code>h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code>. <code>&gt;&gt;&gt; 16</code> slides the bits 16 places to the right, and <code>^</code> (XOR) gives a 1 where the two bits are different.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="collisions">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · When two keys clash</p>
      <h2>Collisions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>There are only so many buckets (16 to start) but endless possible keys, so sometimes two different keys get the same bucket index.</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Collision</p>
        <p>A <b>collision</b> happens when two <b>different</b> keys get the <b>same bucket index</b>. It is normal and expected, and the map must still keep both keys.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Here is a famous one: <b>"Aa"</b> and <b>"BB"</b> have exactly the same hash code.</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Work</th><th>hashCode</th><th>Bucket</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">"Aa"</td><td class="mono">31 × 65 + 97</td><td class="mono">{n(aa)}</td><td class="mono">{bucketIndex(aa)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">"BB"</td><td class="mono">31 × 66 + 66</td><td class="mono">{n(bb)}</td><td class="mono">{bucketIndex(bb)}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The keys are different, so the map must keep <b>both</b>. A common way is <b>chaining</b>: each bucket holds a small <a href={base + '/itc120/linked-lists/'}>linked list</a> (a chain) of entries, one after another. To <code>get</code> "BB", Java goes to bucket {bucketIndex(bb)} and walks its short chain, comparing keys until it finds "BB" (see the picture below). (The <a href={base + '/itc120/hashing/'}>Hashing and hash tables</a> lesson shows more ways to handle collisions, and how to build a hash table yourself.)</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={chainPic()} /><figcaption>Chaining: a bucket can hold more than one entry. <code>get</code> checks only that bucket's chain.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub" id="no-order">
        <h3>Why a HashMap has no order</h3>
        <p>When you loop over a <code>HashMap</code>'s keys, Java walks its buckets in number order (0, 1, 2, …) and reads each chain. So the keys come out in <b>bucket order</b>, not in the order you added them. That is why the <Peek label="Grades program in Part 2" title="Output from Part 2" html={`<pre class="code"><code>${gradesOut}</code></pre>`} /> printed 2025-0101 before 2025-0012: inside Java's real HashMap, 2025-0101 sits in bucket 1 and 2025-0012 in bucket 2.</p>
        <p>Need the keys in the order you added them? Use <code>LinkedHashMap</code>. Sorted by key? Use <code>TreeMap</code>. Compare the three in <Peek label="the three-maps output from Part 2" title="From Part 2 · HashMap, LinkedHashMap, or TreeMap?" html={threePeek} />.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted">Good to know: Java's <code>HashMap</code> uses chaining. If one chain gets very long (8 or more entries, in a big enough table), Java turns that chain into a small red-black tree, so even a crowded bucket stays quick to search.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="lab">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · Look inside</p>
      <h2>HashMap lab</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Try these in order: put <b>CAT → Persian</b>, then <b>DOG → Aspin</b>, then <b>CAT → Puspin</b> (watch the value get replaced). Then put <b>Aa</b> and <b>BB</b> to see a collision. Try <code>get</code> on a key that is not there.</p>
      <MapLab id="maplab" />

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The same thing in Java</h3>
        <p>This program puts three entries and prints each key's hash code and bucket, using the course formula:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(bucketCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt cls="HashMapExample" />
