  <!-- 9 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 9 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-maps" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A map stores key-value pairs. Keys are unique; values can repeat.</li>
          <li><code>put</code> inserts or replaces (and returns the old value, or null), <code>get</code> looks up (or returns null), <code>remove</code> deletes.</li>
          <li>A HashMap hashes the key to pick a bucket, so it can jump straight to it.</li>
          <li>String hashCode: start at 0, then h = 31 × h + code for each character.</li>
          <li>Bucket index (course formula) = hashCode &amp; (capacity − 1) = the last 4 bits when capacity is 16.</li>
          <li>Different keys in the same bucket = a collision; the bucket keeps a chain of entries.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/searching/'}>Linear and binary search</a>, then the two sorting lessons. After them, <a href={base + '/itc120/hashing/'}>Hashing and hash tables</a> builds on this lesson: you build a hash table yourself and learn more ways to handle collisions.</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Oracle, "String.hashCode()," Java SE 21 API documentation (gives the formula s[0]·31<sup>n−1</sup> + … + s[n−1]). <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/String.html#hashCode()">docs.oracle.com</a></li>
          <li>Oracle, "HashMap (Java SE 21 &amp; JDK 21)," Java API documentation (default initial capacity 16). <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/HashMap.html">docs.oracle.com</a></li>
          <li>OpenJDK, <code>java.util.HashMap</code> source code (the <code>hash()</code> method that mixes in the high bits).</li>
          <li>T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest, and C. Stein, <i>Introduction to Algorithms</i>, 4th ed. MIT Press, 2022, Chapter 11 (Hash tables).</li>
          <li>Programiz, "Hash Table." <a href="https://www.programiz.com/dsa/hash-table">programiz.com/dsa/hash-table</a></li>
